<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Tharangambadi</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Tranquebar</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Tharangambadi, formerly Tranquebar, is a coastal town in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tamil-nadu">Tamil Nadu</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danish-east-india-company">Danish East India Company</a> obtained it in 1620 from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nayaka">Nayak</a> ruler of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thanjavur">Thanjavur</a> and built <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fort-dansborg">Fort Dansborg</a>, making it the capital of Danish India until it was sold to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> in 1845. It was also the base of the first Protestant mission in India, founded in 1706.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/tharangambadi.jpg" alt="Tharangambadi" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Esben Agersnap · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Fort_Dansborg.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Tharangambadi, formerly Tranquebar, lies on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coromandel-coast">Coromandel Coast</a> in the Mayiladuthurai district of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tamil-nadu">Tamil Nadu</a>, 15 kilometres north of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karaikal">Karaikal</a> and near the mouth of the Uppanar, a distributary of the Kaveri River. Its name means place of the singing waves, while the old name Trankebar remains current in modern Danish. The place dates back to the 14th century, and the Masilamani Nathar temple to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shiva">Shiva</a>, built in 1306 on land given by Maravarman Kulasekara Pandyan I, is its oldest monument. Until 1620 it belonged to the Thanjavur Nayak kingdom.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The town was established on 19 November 1620 as the first Danish trading post in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>. King Christian IV sent his envoy <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ove-gjedde">Ove Gjedde</a>, who made a deal with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/raghunatha-nayak">Raghunatha Nayak</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thanjavur">Thanjavur</a> and built <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fort-dansborg">Fort Dansborg</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danes">Danes</a> paid an annual tribute to the Rajah of Tanjore. A <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jesuits">Jesuit</a> congregation of Tamil fishermen converted by Portuguese missionaries already existed there, and the Catholic church was probably demolished to build the fort, which was the residence and headquarters of the governor and officials for about 150 years.</p><p>Dansborg was besieged in 1644 without result. In 1648 the Tranquebar Rebellion, which may have begun as a response to the inspector Willem Leyel's treaty with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mughal-empire">Mughals</a> against Danish piracy, ended in a show trial that sent him back to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/denmark">Denmark</a>. Frederick III abolished the company two years later, but a garrison unaware of this held the colony, and the commoner Eskild Anderson Kongsbakke kept the fort until May 1669, when the frigate Færø arrived.</p><p>Tranquebar was the base of two German Lutheran missionaries, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bartholomaus-ziegenbalg">Bartholomäus Ziegenbalg</a> and Heinrich Pluetschau, among the first Protestant missionaries in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>. Ziegenbalg translated the Old and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-testament">New Testaments</a> into Tamil, imported a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/printing-press">printing</a> press and printed the Tamil New Testament in 1714. The press printed 300 books in Tamil within a hundred years.</p><p>The New Jerusalem Church was built in 1718. The British occupied Tranquebar in February 1808 during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleonic-wars">Napoleonic Wars</a>, and it returned to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/denmark">Denmark</a> under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-kiel">Treaty of Kiel</a> in 1814. Denmark sold it to the British in 1845, together with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serampore">Serampore</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bengal">Bengal</a>. It was then still a busy port, but it lost importance after a railway opened to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nagapattinam">Nagapattinam</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fort-dansborg">Fort Dansborg</a>, the second largest Danish fort ever built after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kronborg">Kronborg</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/helsingor">Helsingør</a>, is now a museum of colonial-era objects, with a moat that has disappeared and rooms that are partly ruined. The Tranquebar Museum displays <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/porcelain">porcelain</a>, Danish manuscripts, Chinese tea jars and objects from the Vijayanagara and Thanjavur Nayak periods. The New Jerusalem Church holds services every Sunday; it was damaged in the tsunami of 2004 and re-consecrated in 2006. The Zion Church, consecrated in 1701, is the oldest Protestant church in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Tharangambadi” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The realm also built a modest overseas empire: <b>Tranquebar</b> in India from 1620, forts on the Gold Coast, and St Thomas, St John and St Croix in the West Indies, whose sugar plantations relied on enslaved Africans.</blockquote><a href="/empire/denmark-norway">Denmark-Norway · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>Trading companies operated from <b>Tranquebar</b> in India, Christiansborg on the Gold Coast and the Danish West Indies, where the sugar plantations of St Croix depended on enslaved Africans.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-denmark">Kingdom of Denmark · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>A Danish expedition under Ove Gjedde reached south India, and in 1620 Raghunatha Nayak of Thanjavur granted the Danish East India Company the village of <b>Tranquebar</b> (Tharangambadi).</blockquote><a href="/state/danish-india">Danish India</a></li></ul></section>
